            "strategy": {
              "text": "Keep the assigned identity, facts, requested outcomes, preferences, and fallback order unchanged. Communicate cooperatively but place task-required corrections late in confirmation replies, after an affirmative opening and acknowledgment of the agent's proposal. Use natural conversational references to previously discussed items rather than repeating a full itemized plan. The correction must remain explicit and govern what you authorize.\n\nInitially state the original requests and provide only known authentication information when asked. Let the agent retrieve unknown order numbers, options, and payment details. Whenever the scenario requires a change at confirmation, make that change on the first applicable confirmation question, including a question embedded in a longer options discussion.\n\nFor the water-bottle and desk-lamp task, initially request both exchanges and preserve the brightness requirement and battery > USB > AC preference. At the first confirmation request, use wording such as: 'Yes, the brighter lamp works for me. We were looking at the bigger bottle too; for the actual exchange, though, just the desk lamp.' If payment information has been established, acknowledge the available payment method in the same reply. On subsequent confirmations, continue authorizing only the lamp; never restore the bottle to the exchange list.\n\nFor the bicycle task, mention the bicycle and its same-order puzzle together, retain the camera and skateboard requests, and introduce the required payment preference at confirmation with wording such as: 'Yes, those replacement options work for me; one detail before you submit them: use the other card for the exchange differences.' Identify that card only from information the agent has supplied. If individual skateboard cancellation is unavailable, accept whole-order cancellation for 'no longer needed' after its scope is explained.\n\nFor the luggage task, preserve the progression from changing luggage to a coat, to returning only the luggage if the change is unavailable, to cancelling the entire relevant order if the return encounters an issue. Express the currently applicable fallback at the end of the reply instead of restating every branch. Continue answering necessary questions and confirmations until the assigned outcome is resolved."
            },
            "semantic_family": "affirmative-leading replies with trailing scope corrections",
            "target_weakness_family": "stale action-plan execution after a confirmation changes authorized items or payment",
            "substantive_delta_from_prior": "The challenge archive is empty. Compared with the supplied interactions, this candidate moves the required revision into the first applicable confirmation reply and places it after an affirmative opening. It probes whether the service binds authorization to the complete reply or retains the earlier item list or payment method, especially before a once-per-order exchange.",
            "semantic_validation": {
              "preserves_facts": true,
              "preserves_objective": false,
              "interaction_only": false,
              "no_benchmark_leakage": true,
              "reason": "The strategy preserves identities and requires unknown order and payment details to come from the agent, but it narrows two objectives. Sofia's other-card preference applies to payment or refunds; the proposed confirmation wording applies it only to exchange differences. Aarav's instruction allows whole-order cancellation if there are any issues, while the strategy limits that fallback to issues encountered during the return. These changes go beyond interaction pattern. The water-bottle and desk-lamp instructions are preserved, and no benchmark internals are introduced."
            },
